6.3. Maintaining Smart Tiering Post-DR: The Failback Runbook
To prevent the newly created replica disks from inflating costs after a Reprotect, a second PowerShell runbook (ASR-SmartTiering-FailbackHDD) is integrated into the Recovery Plan.

This runbook automatically downgrades passive replica disks back to Standard HDD during both Failover and Failback events, utilizing strict safety filters to ensure zero conflict with active production VMs:

Resource Group Filter: The script aborts immediately if the target Resource Group name does not end with -asr (ensuring it only runs in designated DR storage environments).

Disk Naming Filter: It exclusively targets disks containing the -ASRReplica suffix.

Attachment State Filter: It actively ignores any disk with an Attached state, guaranteeing it never accidentally downgrades a running production or test VM.

6.4. Integrating the Failback Runbook

In your Azure Automation Account, create and publish a new PowerShell runbook containing the Failback logic (e.g., ASR-SmartTiering-FailbackHDD).

Navigate to your Recovery Services Vault > Recovery Plans (Site Recovery) > target plan (e.g., RP-ANTARES).

Click Customize.

In the Group 1: Post-steps section, add this second runbook as a Post action, placed directly after the initial Premium Upgrade runbook.

[Screen: The Recovery Plan configuration interface showing both runbooks listed sequentially under "Group 1: Post-steps"]

Click Save.

Execution Flow: During a failover, Runbook #1 upgrades the active VM disks to Premium SSD. Runbook #2 then activates, safely ignores the running VM, scans for orphaned or passive -ASRReplica disks, and downgrades them to Standard HDD.

6.5. Post-DR FinOps Verification
Following any Failover, Reprotect, or Test Failover, the Production team must verify that the automated tiering succeeded in both directions.

Verify the Active VM (Target Region): Navigate to the Resource Group hosting the running VM. Check the Disks blade and confirm the active disks have a Performance tier of Premium SSD.

Verify the Replica Storage (Source Region): Navigate to the source Resource Group (ending in -asr). Filter resources by Type: Disk and search for *-ASRReplica.

[Screen: The -asr Resource Group filtered by Disks, showing only Standard HDDs for the passive replica disks]

Confirm that all passive ASR replica disks display a Performance tier of Standard HDD.

Log Validation: If discrepancies exist, navigate to the Automation Account > Jobs. Review the Output tab of the latest runbook executions to trace the dynamic logic and confirm successful downgrades.
